---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import marquee from '../../styles/components/marquee.css?url';
import process from '../../styles/components/process.css?url';
import tabs from '../../styles/components/tabs.css?url';
import page from '../../styles/projects/echo.css?url';
---

<ProjectLayout slug="echo" styles={[marquee, process, tabs, page]}>
  <div class="cover">
    <figure class="cv-img">
      <img
        src="/media/echo/cover.webp"
        alt="Echo: three sprout characters running through a sunny meadow past stone ruins"
      />
    </figure>
    <div class="cv-deco" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="cv-ring"></span>
      <svg class="cv-ln" viewBox="0 0 106 30">
        <polyline class="cl" points="4.3,1.2 105.4,29.5"></polyline>
      </svg>
      <span class="x cv-x1"></span>
      <span class="x cv-x2"></span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sec first" id="s-overview" data-sec aria-labelledby="h-overview">
    <div class="sh">
      <h2 class="sh-t" id="h-overview">
        Overview
      </h2>
      <span class="sh-l" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="x sh-x" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-a">
        <p class="lead">A shared forest where two children can find each other, without being told to.</p>
        <p class="tx">
          Echo is a two-player forest world with no score, no win condition, no dialogue and no instructions. Most
          inclusive games treat social interaction as a skill to teach children with ADHD. Echo takes the opposite
          route: it redesigns the environment itself, so interaction between children with ADHD and their neurotypical
          peers can emerge on its own.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-b">
        <span class="vl" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <dl class="facts">
          <div>
            <dt>Supervision</dt>
            <dd>
              Prof. Giorgio Buratti
              <br />
              Prof. Giuseppe Amoruso
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Built with</dt>
            <dd>Unity, C#, Netcode for GameObjects</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Players</dt>
            <dd>Multiplayer, joined by a room code</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="s-question" data-sec aria-labelledby="h-question">
    <div class="sh">
      <h2 class="sh-t" id="h-question">
        Research question
      </h2>
      <span class="sh-l" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="x sh-x" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    <p class="rq">
      <span class="rq-l" aria-hidden="true"></span>How can game design create environments where social interaction
      emerges naturally between children with ADHD and their neurotypical peers, without instruction, predefined roles
      or performance-based expectations?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="s-approach" data-sec aria-labelledby="h-approach">
    <div class="sh">
      <h2 class="sh-t" id="h-approach">
        Approach
      </h2>
      <span class="sh-l" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="x sh-x" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <p class="lead">Design-based research, from the literature to two real play sessions.</p>
      <p class="tx">
        The study followed a double diamond process. A literature review and a semi-structured interview with a
        professional who works directly with children with ADHD led to a design framework. The framework became a game
        concept and a working multiplayer prototype, which I then observed in use with two pairs of children.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="dg rv"
      role="group"
      aria-label="Process: from the research question, through a literature review and an expert interview, to a design framework; then through a game concept and a multiplayer prototype, to two play sessions"
    >
      <div style="position: relative; width: 880px; height: 300px">
        <svg
          width="880"
          height="300"
          viewBox="0 0 880 300"
          aria-hidden="true"
          style="position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: 880px; height: 300px; overflow: visible"
